@page "/docs/icons"

<DocsPage Title="Icons" Description="Pick the icon set your components draw their icons from. add installs its package.">
    <p>
        The components draw their icons with the <code>Icon</code> component, from the package of an icon set. You pick the set
        the way shadcn/ui&apos;s <code>iconLibrary</code> does: <code>add</code> installs that set&apos;s package and copies each
        component with the set&apos;s icons, as <code>&lt;Icon Data="…" /&gt;</code> elements. For icons of your own, each set is
        also a NuGet package.
    </p>

    <DocsHeading Title="Icon libraries" />
    <DocsTable>
        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Library</th><th><code>iconLibrary</code></th><th>Package</th><th>Icons&apos; license</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                @foreach (var (name, url, key, package, license) in Libraries)
                {
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="row"><a href="@url" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">@name</a></th>
                        <td><code>@key</code></td>
                        <td><code>@package</code></td>
                        <td>@license</td>
                    </tr>
                }
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </DocsTable>
    <p>
        Lucide is the default, and the one these docs show. The components use the same icons in each set as shadcn/ui does.
        Font Awesome, which shadcn/ui doesn&apos;t offer, uses its free solid icons.
    </p>

    <DocsHeading Title="Choosing a library" />
    <p>
        Pass <code>--icon-library</code> to <code>init</code>. It sets <a href="/docs/blazix-json#iconlibrary"><code>iconLibrary</code></a>
        in <code>blazix.json</code>, which you can also edit by hand, and installs that set&apos;s package.
    </p>
    <CliCommand Args="init --icon-library tabler" />
    <p>
        From then on, <code>add</code> copies each component with that set&apos;s icons, and installs the package if the project
        doesn&apos;t have it yet. Each icon keeps the classes and attributes the component gives it, so it sizes and colors the
        same way in every set. In the Accordion&apos;s trigger, the copy has:
    </p>
    <CodeFigure Code="@CopiedCode" Title="Components/UI/Accordion/AccordionTrigger.razor" />
    <p>
        <code>add</code> takes no <code>--icon-library</code> of its own. It uses the library in <code>blazix.json</code> and refuses
        a different one, so a project never mixes sets by accident.
    </p>

    <DocsHeading Title="Changing library" />
    <p>
        For components you&apos;ve already added, run <code>migrate icons</code>. It asks which libraries to migrate from and to,
        unless you pass <code>--from</code> and <code>--to</code>, then asks you to confirm. Once you do, it moves their icons,
        installs the new library&apos;s package, and sets <code>iconLibrary</code>, so later adds match.
    </p>
    <CliCommand Args="migrate icons --to phosphor" />
    <p><strong>Options</strong></p>
    <CodeFigure Code="@MigrateOptionsCode" Language="text" />
    <p>
        With a path, it migrates just those files and leaves <code>blazix.json</code> alone. Each icon with no equivalent in the new
        library is listed by name and left as it is. The CLI pairs the icons its components use, so an icon of your own that no
        component uses has no equivalent here, even where the new library has one. Migrate those by hand, or keep them.
    </p>
    <p>
        Components copied by an earlier version draw their icons inline, as SVG. <code>migrate icons</code> moves only
        <code>&lt;Icon&gt;</code>s, so those copies stay as they are. To get the icon component instead, add the component again with
        <code>--overwrite</code>: the copy is replaced with the current one, which uses <code>&lt;Icon&gt;</code>s from your library.
    </p>

    <DocsHeading Title="Icons of your own" />
    <p>Each set is a package of its own. Add the one that matches your components, or any other:</p>
    <CodeFigure Code="@PackageCode" Language="bash" />
    <p>
        Then render an icon with <code>&lt;Icon&gt;</code>, from <code>Blazix.Icons</code>, which the set&apos;s package brings with it.
        Put the two <code>@@using</code> lines in <code>_Imports.razor</code> to use icons everywhere, as the examples in these docs do:
    </p>
    <CodeFigure Code="@UsageCode" Title="Pages/Files.razor" LineNumbers="true" />
    <ul>
        <li>
            Without <code>Label</code>, an icon is decoration and is hidden from assistive technology (<code>aria-hidden="true"</code>).
            Use it next to text that says the same thing.
        </li>
        <li>
            With <code>Label</code>, it&apos;s an image with that name (<code>role="img"</code> and <code>aria-label</code>). Give one to an
            icon that&apos;s the only content of a button or link, so the control has a name.
        </li>
        <li>
            It takes the text color (<code>currentColor</code>). Attributes you pass, such as <code>class</code>, <code>style</code> and
            <code>width</code>, go on the <code>&lt;svg&gt;</code> and win over its defaults. <code>StrokeWidth</code> changes an outline
            icon&apos;s stroke, which defaults to 2.
        </li>
        <li>
            Each icon is a property, so a typo is a compile error, and your editor completes the names. Trimming, which Blazor
            WebAssembly does when you publish, keeps only the icons your app uses: an app that uses two Lucide icons ships a few
            kilobytes of icon data, not the whole set.
        </li>
        <li>
            An icon that points along the line, such as an arrow or a chevron, should turn around in right-to-left layouts. Add
            <code>rtl:rotate-180</code> to its classes. The components&apos; own icons do this already (see <a href="/docs/rtl">RTL</a>).
        </li>
    </ul>

    <DocsHeading Title="Licenses" />
    <p>
        Blazix&apos;s code is MIT. The icons keep their own licenses, listed above. Each package carries its set&apos;s license as
        <code>ICONS-LICENSE.txt</code>. Lucide&apos;s icons are ISC, except the ones it took from Feather, which are MIT.
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>Font Awesome Free</strong>: its icons are CC BY 4.0, which requires attribution. Each package carries the
            attribution in <code>ICONS-LICENSE.txt</code>, and so do the CLI&apos;s notices. The components name the package&apos;s
            icons and carry no SVG of their own.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Remix Icon</strong>: the package and the components use Remix Icon 4.8.0, its last release under Apache-2.0.
            From 4.9.0 Remix Icon has a license of its own, the Remix Icon License v1.0. That license doesn&apos;t allow distributing
            the icons as an icon library, which the package would be. Icons that Remix Icon added after 4.8.0 aren&apos;t included.
        </li>
    </ul>
</DocsPage>

@code {
    private static readonly (string Name, string Url, string Key, string Package, string License)[] Libraries =
    [
        ("Lucide", "https://lucide.dev/icons", "lucide", "Blazix.Icons.Lucide", "ISC"),
        ("Tabler Icons", "https://tabler.io/icons", "tabler", "Blazix.Icons.Tabler", "MIT"),
        ("Hugeicons", "https://hugeicons.com/icons", "hugeicons", "Blazix.Icons.Hugeicons", "MIT (the free set)"),
        ("Phosphor Icons", "https://phosphoricons.com", "phosphor", "Blazix.Icons.Phosphor", "MIT"),
        ("Remix Icon", "https://remixicon.com", "remixicon", "Blazix.Icons.Remixicon", "Apache-2.0 (4.8.0)"),
        ("Font Awesome Free", "https://fontawesome.com/search?ic=free", "fontawesome", "Blazix.Icons.FontAwesome", "CC BY 4.0"),
    ];

    private const string CopiedCode = """
        <Icon Data="TablerIcons.ChevronDown"
              aria-hidden="true" data-slot="accordion-trigger-icon" class="pointer-events-none shrink-0 group-aria-expanded/accordion-trigger:hidden" />
        """;

    private const string MigrateOptionsCode = """
        Usage: blazix migrate icons [path] [options]

        Arguments:
          path                   a file or folder to migrate (default: componentsDir)

        Options:
          --from <library>       the library the icons come from (default: blazix.json's iconLibrary)
          --to <library>         the library to migrate to, which must differ from --from: lucide, tabler, hugeicons, phosphor, remixicon, fontawesome
          -y, --yes              migrate without the confirmation
          -p, --project <path>   the project, when the folder has more than one
        """;

    private const string PackageCode = """
        dotnet add package Blazix.Icons.Lucide
        """;

    private const string UsageCode = """
        @using Blazix.Icons
        @using Blazix.Icons.Lucide

        <Button>
            <Icon Data="LucideIcons.Plus" />
            New file
        </Button>

        <Button Variant="ButtonVariant.Outline" Size="ButtonSize.Icon">
            <Icon Data="LucideIcons.Settings" Label="Settings" />
        </Button>
        """;
}
